# session.schema — which layer may decide each key of the `session` subsystem.
#
# Read by /usr/lib/w/w-conf-lib.sh (and its Python twin) during the load
# pass; a user-layer assignment to a `system` key is dropped with a warning, and
# `wconf_set` refuses to write it in the first place. See w-conf.md.
#
# Format: <KEY>\t<scope>       scope: system | user | both
#   system — root domain: the admin (or fleet policy) decides, per machine.
#   user   — the logged-in user may override it in ~/.config/w/session.conf.
#   both   — either layer may speak; the usual priority order applies.
#
# Every key here is `user`, for the same reason as `nightlight`: the whole
# subsystem lives inside one graphical session. It reads that user's own window
# list, writes that user's own snapshot (~/.local/state/w/session.json, mode
# 0600), and its daemon is a `systemctl --user` unit. There is no machine-wide
# artifact to protect, so there is no polkit anywhere in this subsystem — the
# Hub calls `w-session` directly, exactly as it calls `w-nightlight`.
#
# A fleet that does want to fix these (a kiosk that must always come up clean,
# say) still can: policy.d sits above the user layer and locks the setters,
# which is the mechanism for exactly this case.

MODE	user
AUTOSAVE_SEC	user
EXCLUDE_CLASSES	user
MAX_WINDOWS	user
TERMINAL_APPS	user
